WEBVTT

1
00:00:05.507 --> 00:00:12.545
When guns shattered the peace of Europe in August 1914, they signaled the beginning of what was then called the Great War.

2
00:00:12.546 --> 00:00:28.875
Over the next five years, almost ten million soldiers lost their lives in this global conflict, which pitted the Central Powers of Germany, Austria-Hungary, Turkey, and Bulgaria against the Allied Forces led by Britain, France, Russia, and, from 1917, the United States.

3
00:00:28.876 --> 00:00:38.615
In addition to the soldiers who perished, another twenty million were wounded, and approximately 13 million civilians lost their lives due to disease, starvation, and military action.

4
00:00:38.616 --> 00:00:50.495
The assassination of the Austrian heir to the throne, Franz Ferdinand, and his wife in Sarajevo on June 28, 1914, by a Serbian nationalist became the spark that set off the war.

5
00:00:50.496 --> 00:00:55.503
But for years, Europe had been a powder keg waiting for such a spark to ignite it.

6
00:00:55.504 --> 00:01:07.366
Both Germany and Britain had overseas colonies and wanted to control the international seas in order to ensure the transportation of raw materials, such as lumber and minerals, from their territories.

7
00:01:07.367 --> 00:01:17.436
In 1906, the naval rivalry between Germany and Britain intensified when the British launched the HMS Dreadnought, a huge battleship that made previous ones obsolete.

8
00:01:17.437 --> 00:01:22.930
The treaties and alliances that many nations had formed also contributed to European instability.

9
00:01:22.931 --> 00:01:31.034
In 1879, Germany and Austria-Hungary had signed the Dual Alliance, in which they agreed to help each other if attacked by Russia.

10
00:01:31.035 --> 00:01:38.075
In 1904, Britain and France signed the Entente Cordiale, an agreement to support each other's policies.

11
00:01:38.076 --> 00:01:41.976
In 1907, Russia signed a similar agreement with Britain.

12
00:01:41.977 --> 00:01:51.566
In 1912, Britain and France signed the Anglo-French Naval Convention, in which Britain promised to protect France's coastline in case of German naval attack.

13
00:01:51.567 --> 00:01:58.446
Two quick wars in the Balkans, in 1912 and 1913, only added to the tension.

14
00:01:58.447 --> 00:02:01.731
The days after the assassination were a countdown to war.

15
00:02:01.732 --> 00:02:13.164
On July 23, Austria-Hungary sent Serbia an ultimatum intended to humiliate the nation, in hopes that the ultimatum would be rejected and provide an excuse to go to war.

16
00:02:13.165 --> 00:02:21.649
Even though Serbia agreed to most of Austria-Hungary's demands, Austria-Hungary declared war on Serbia within days, on July 28.

17
00:02:21.650 --> 00:02:29.767
Russia, bound to Serbia by a treaty, mobilized its army in support. On August 1, Germany declared war on Russia.

18
00:02:29.768 --> 00:02:38.425
Two days later, Germany declared war on France. On August 4, German troops invaded neutral Belgium, taking the shortest route to Paris.

19
00:02:38.426 --> 00:02:44.352
England, bound by an old treaty to protect Belgian neutrality, declared war against Germany.

20
00:02:44.353 --> 00:02:51.739
Although many people optimistically thought the war would be short and the soldiers would be "home by Christmas," the truth was quite different.

21
00:02:51.740 --> 00:02:57.678
Troops on both sides dug themselves into trenches for protection from the destructive power of machine guns.

22
00:02:57.679 --> 00:03:05.385
Rolls of barbed wire added to the danger of crossing "no man's land," as the area between the opposing sides' trenches was called.

23
00:03:05.386 --> 00:03:19.715
Along the western front, the Belgians occupied 65 kilometers of trenches; the British, 145; and the French, the remaining 560 kilometers of trenches between the English Channel and the border of Switzerland, which was neutral.

24
00:03:19.716 --> 00:03:28.549
By the end of 1914, soldiers on both sides had dug almost ten thousand kilometers of trenches—which would be their muddy homes for the next four years.

25
00:03:28.550 --> 00:03:30.930
Life in the trenches, however, was brutal.

26
00:03:30.931 --> 00:03:40.371
Thousands of soldiers died of diseases such as dysentery and pneumonia, and suffered from lice and trench foot, a disease caused by the feet being constantly wet.

27
00:03:40.372 --> 00:03:48.546
Thousands of soldiers died each day in futile attacks when they were required to charge into machine-gun fire to try to gain a few meters of land.

28
00:03:48.547 --> 00:03:55.076
Military leaders were painfully slow to realize that in order to break the deadlock, they had to turn to new technology.

29
00:03:55.077 --> 00:04:02.408
Such old-fashioned aids as horses (for transport) and carrier pigeons (to deliver messages) were used in this war.

30
00:04:02.409 --> 00:04:08.728
But this was the first war in which poison gases such as chlorine were used, with deadly results.

31
00:04:08.729 --> 00:04:22.336
This was also the first war in which airplanes played a role; from the open-air cockpits of planes, pilots could use handheld firearms and machine guns against each other, drop bombs, and gather information about the enemy.

32
00:04:22.337 --> 00:04:30.828
The most impressive military invention, however, was the armored tank, a massive metal vehicle that could climb over obstacles and cross trenches.

33
00:04:30.829 --> 00:04:37.819
In April 1917, at the Battle of Cambrai, the British proved that tanks could be used to advance the line of battle.

34
00:04:37.820 --> 00:04:42.651
The Germans introduced a new type of deadly naval warfare with their submarines.

35
00:04:42.652 --> 00:04:49.205
These vessels, called U-boats, traveled underwater undetected and could launch torpedoes at enemy ships.

36
00:04:49.206 --> 00:04:59.799
On May 7, 1915, the British passenger ship Lusitania was sunk by a German U-boat off the coast of Ireland, and more than a thousand civilians died.

37
00:04:59.800 --> 00:05:10.251
The sinking of the Lusitania enraged many people in the Allied countries and helped sway opinion in the United States, which was officially neutral, in favor of joining the Allies.

38
00:05:10.252 --> 00:05:15.906
In March 1917, German U-boats sank three U.S. merchant ships with heavy loss of life.

39
00:05:15.907 --> 00:05:24.606
Finally, on April 6, U.S. president Woodrow Wilson declared war on Germany, citing its "wanton and wholesale destruction of innocent lives."

40
00:05:24.607 --> 00:05:32.115
By fall of 1918, the Allied Forces had gained the upper hand; the German army and the Central Powers had collapsed.

41
00:05:32.116 --> 00:05:39.275
In November, the German emperor abdicated and fled to neutral Holland, leaving his country in a state of civil unrest.

42
00:05:39.276 --> 00:05:49.444
At 11:00 a.m. on November 11, 1918 (the eleventh hour of the eleventh day of the eleventh month), a cease-fire went into effect and the war was over.

43
00:05:49.445 --> 00:05:52.762
The European continent, however, would never be the same.

44
00:05:52.763 --> 00:06:04.474
In Russia, military defeat, starvation, and mutiny in the army had resulted in the overthrow of the czar, who was executed along with his family, and a group of communists took over the country.

45
00:06:04.475 --> 00:06:13.748
The mighty empire of Austria-Hungary dissolved, becoming new, separate republics: Austria, Hungary, Czechoslovakia, Poland, and Yugoslavia.

46
00:06:13.749 --> 00:06:20.528
Estonia, Latvia, Lithuania, and Finland also gained their independence from their previous rulers.

47
00:06:20.529 --> 00:06:26.012
In December 1918, leaders from all the nations met in Paris to negotiate a peace treaty.

48
00:06:26.013 --> 00:06:37.773
The terms of the Treaty of Versailles were harsh, stating that Germany must accept moral responsibility for starting the war, pay war reparations to the Allies, and give up its territories overseas.

49
00:06:37.774 --> 00:06:44.549
Its armed forces were also limited to 100,000 people, and the country was not allowed to produce munitions.

50
00:06:44.550 --> 00:06:51.929
The treaty was finally signed on June 28, 1919, exactly five years after the assassination of Franz Ferdinand.

51
00:06:51.930 --> 00:06:58.425
But the unfortunate result of the treaty was that it set the stage for a second massive war only twenty years later.

52
00:06:58.426 --> 00:07:04.054
Sadly, the peace following what had been called "the war to end all wars" was a short one.
